32:["$","p",null,{"children":["This is the hook for host-level conditions the scheduler cannot see: disk\nfilling, a shared license pool exhausted, a GPU in a bad state. It costs a\nprocess spawn per action, so keep the script trivial. And note the\n",["$","code",null,{"children":"experimental_"}]," prefix: it is not covered by stability guarantees."]}]
33:["$","$L7",null,{"as":"h2","id":"heterogeneous-pools","children":"Heterogeneous pools"}]
34:["$","p",null,{"children":["One scheduler can route to pools of different shapes; the mechanism is platform\nproperties, and it is covered in full on\n",["$","$L6",null,{"href":"/remote-execution/platform-properties","children":"platform properties"}],". The scaling point\nis narrower: ",["$","strong",null,{"children":"each pool is a separate scaling unit with its own queue-depth\nseries"}],", because ",["$","code",null,{"children":"forge:queue_depth"}]," groups by instance and priority, not\nby which pool could serve the work."]}]
35:["$","p",null,{"children":"If you scale a mixed fleet on one aggregate number, a backlog of GPU actions\nwill scale up your compile pool and change nothing. Either split the recording\nrule by the property that distinguishes the pools, or run a scheduler instance\nper pool. The second is blunter and easier to reason about."}]
36:["$","$L7",null,{"as":"h2","id":"when-it-doesnt-work","children":"When it doesn't work"}]
37:["$","$L4f",null,{"children":[["$","$L50",null,{"title":"Queue depth is high but workers are not busy","id":"queue-depth-is-high-but-workers-are-not-busy","children":["$","p",null,{"children":["Actions are queued that no worker matches. This is nearly always a platform\nproperty mismatch: an action declaring something no worker advertises, or a\nscheduler key configured as ",["$","code",null,{"children":"priority"}]," (which constrains nothing) when it should\nbe ",["$","code",null,{"children":"exact"}]," or ",["$","code",null,{"children":"minimum"}],". Adding workers will not help. Check the scheduler's\nworker-match logging, and see\n",["$","$L6",null,{"href":"/remote-execution/platform-properties","children":"platform properties"}],".\n",["$","$L6",null,{"href":"/operate/troubleshooting#actions-queue-forever-and-workers-sit-idle","children":"Troubleshooting"}],"\nhas the log lines that distinguish \"nothing matches\" from \"everything is busy\",\nand why a bad property ",["$","em",null,{"children":"value"}]," hangs where a bad property ",["$","em",null,{"children":"name"}]," errors."]}]}],["$","$L50",null,{"title":"Adding workers doesn't make builds faster","id":"adding-workers-doesnt-make-builds-faster","children":["$","p",null,{"children":["The bottleneck is downstream. Workers pull inputs from and push outputs to the\nCAS, so a saturated CAS or a saturated network means more workers just wait in a\ndifferent place. Check CAS latency as you scale, and check\n",["$","code",null,{"children":"connections_per_endpoint"}]," on the workers' ",["$","code",null,{"children":"grpc"}]," store; the default of 1 makes\na single connection the ceiling."]}]}],["$","$L50",null,{"title":"Workers keep being marked dead and their actions requeued","id":"workers-keep-being-marked-dead-and-their-actions-requeued","children":["$","p",null,{"children":[["$","code",null,{"children":"worker_timeout_s"}]," defaults to ",["$","strong",null,{"children":"10 seconds"}],", checked once a second. That is a\nshort leash and it is deliberate (fast detection means fast requeue), but a\nworker whose host is starved for longer than that, or whose network hiccups,\nwill be evicted. If you see churn without actual failures, raise it. The cost\nof raising it is a longer stall before a genuinely dead worker's actions are\nretried. Releases up to 1.7.2 defaulted to 5 seconds and only counted\nheartbeats, so a worker busy reporting results could still be evicted."]}]}],["$","$L50",null,{"title":"Setting `max_job_retries: 0` didn't stop retries","id":"setting-max_job_retries-0-didnt-stop-retries","children":["$","p",null,{"children":["A configured ",["$","code",null,{"children":"0"}]," is read as unset and falls back to the default of ",["$","strong",null,{"children":"3"}],". The\nfield cannot express \"never retry\" through that field. If an action must not be\nretried, that has to be handled above Forge."]}]}],["$","$L50",null,{"title":"A long action fails with an invalid-argument error about timeouts","id":"a-long-action-fails-with-an-invalid-argument-error-about-timeouts","children":["$","p",null,{"children":[["$","code",null,{"children":"max_action_timeout_s"}]," on the worker defaults to ",["$","strong",null,{"children":"20 minutes"}],", and a client\nrequesting a longer timeout is ",["$","strong",null,{"children":"rejected outright"}]," rather than clamped down to\nthe maximum. Long link steps and EDA runs need this raised on the worker before\nthe client asks for it. (The field also accepts the older spelling\n",["$","code",null,{"children":"max_action_timeout"}],".)"]}]}],["$","$L50",null,{"title":"Workers exhaust file descriptors under load","id":"workers-exhaust-file-descriptors-under-load","children":["$","p",null,{"children":["The process-wide open-file semaphore defaults to 24,576, which a worker running\nmany concurrent actions over a filesystem store will reach. Raise ","$L51","\nunder ","$L52",", and raise the OS ","$L53"," limit to at least match; the\nsemaphore does not know what the kernel will allow."]}]}],"$L54","$L55"]}]
